Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A kinase can be activated if it is phosphorylated, or bound to a secondary messenger, cyclin, or calcium ion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A kinase, in addition to being controlled by being phosphorylated, can also be controlled via the use of a secondary messenger, cyclin, or calcium ion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for the function of kinases, since by having multiple means of activation, it ensures that the kinases are only active in the right environments.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers\">In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers</a><ul><li>This shows how only one type of signal receptors need kinases.</li></ul></li></ul>","A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A small and apparently incidental event can be constructed so as to contain the shape of the larger conflict, which lets a work signal its direction without announcing it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is how a reader is given a deeper understanding of a theme directly rather than through exposition, and it is a reliable marker of authorial control.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hardy uses the device throughout. When the two wagoners collide in <em>The Mayor of Casterbridge</em>, one hired by Henchard and one by Farfrae, the collision is the whole struggle between the old and the new in miniature.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a><ul><li>The same compression operating across scenes rather than within one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>The claim about life that this technique depends on being true.</li></ul></li></ul>","An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An action that is the result of ignorance is only excusable given that the ignorant action regarding something particular, not when it is universal and not when it is voluntary ignorance, regardless of the type of ignorance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle defines clearly the types of ignorance and whether they are considered morally repugnant.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Ignorance of the particular: when you shoot a rabbit, but it turns out the rabbit was actually someone’s foot. You are distressed by your actions, and you repent. Here, you were ignorant regarding the particular situation, though you are not ignorant of the universal, i.e., that shooting someone is bad.</li><li>Ignorance of the universal: you shoot someone because you don’t like the way they look. In this case, you are ignorant of the universal moral principle at play.</li><li>Voluntary ignorance: you voluntarily got drunk, and then killed someone in a fight. Here, you could have chosen not to get drunk, so the consequences of the action falls on the you.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards\">An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards</a><ul><li>The ignorance being referred to here is ignorance of the particular.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>The ignorance being referred to here is also ignorance of the particular.</li></ul></li></ul>","Authoritarian & non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Satellite night light data shows that authoritarian or non-democratic nations overstate their GDP growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><strong>Autocracy Gradient</strong>: As a country becomes more authoritarian, the same increase in nightlight level translates into higher levels of GDP growth.<ul><li>The gradient is larger before an election, or when growth is low.</li></ul></li><li>GDP growth in authoritarian nations are typically overstated by a factor of 1.15 to 1.3.<ul><li>Over the long term, this represents a 40% inflation of GDP.</li></ul></li><li>China is a major example of this case.<ul><li>Even beyond analysis with nightlights, analysis of GDP growth through the construction of an Engels curve showed the curve shifting over time, indicating overstating and understating inflation by the government over time.<ul><li>The Engel Curve analysis showed the growth was actually underestimated in the 90s, but over estimated from the 2000s onwards. </li><li>In total, the Engel-Adjusted real growth of the Chinese economy has fallen below the official growth reported by the Chinese government, in largely part to the fall in growth during the 2008-2009 crisis.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing\">GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing</a><ul><li>It can be manipulated, for instance.</li></ul></li></ul>","Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The theory that capitalism leads people to abandon morality, become greedier, promotes consumerism, and alienates people. Additionally, civic bonds are weakened and civic virtue declines.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the theory of self-destruction that describes a potential outcome of market capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>This is the opposite.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Cholesterol is a type of steroid that forms the basis for many different types of hormones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Cholesterol is therefore not merely structural. The same molecule that regulates membrane fluidity is the precursor of the steroid hormones, so one lipid links the physics of the membrane to endocrine signaling.</p>","Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For Chinese people, they do not consider a political discontinuity to consist in being conquered by outsiders. Rather, they consider it in terms of cultural continuation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents insight into the way that the Chinese view their state and their views on the importance of culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Even though the Chinese state was taken over my Mongols, and then the Manchus, the Chinese people consider these ethnically different dynasties to be “Chinese” dynasties, since they shared the same culture that Chinese people did (at least, eventually).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>It seems that this note affirms the linked note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered\">The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered</a><ul><li>Thus the Mongols adapted to Chinese culture?</li></ul></li></ul>","Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Congress is the only branch of government that has “intrabranch checks,” meaning that its power is checked by its internal parts. This comes in the form of the upper and lower houses, which may disagree about something and thus put a check on the power of Congress as a whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The intrabranch checks on power are important, as they ensure that Congress does not get too power, especially if the other branches of government are ever severely weakened.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li></ul>","Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Continuity of history is something that needs to be actively pursued. Although religion is not something that is by necessity required in order for our preservation of history, the best way to do so in the age of modernity is through ascetic religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Burckhardt’s views on how we are to preserve our history, i.e., through “true” ascetic religion.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Burckhardt does not argue that there is a theology of history, as he dismisses other such unifying theories of history. However, he holds on to his belief in continuity, which, in his view, requires an active maintenance of the past. This, he argues, is incompatible with the unfettered greed and power-hungriness that permeates the modern world in the form of capitalism, nationalism, militarism, etc. What is required, he avers, is “spiritual practice and pure contemplation.” Therefore, he argues that ascetic religion is required in order for history to be continued. And in doing so, he rejects modern Christianity, which he sees as being a religion that lacks any of its original spiritual commitments, and is now a mere action out of habit, exactly opposite his idea of active continuity.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 1, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 30</p><p>The moral strength of the early Christians consisted not least in their unconcern with nature and culture; dominated by an eschatological faith, the Christians of the apostolic age could not have any real interest in them. <strong>\"The end of the world and eternity were at the door, and it was easy to turn away from the world and its delights…”</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>In the modern Christianity, Burckhardt argues that this is gone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>This is in part what Burckhardt argues.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>This is part of what he argues.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>It is this divine calling that makes Christianity spiritual in Burckhardt’s view?</li></ul></li></ul>","Free will is the capacity to choose the good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the oldest conception of free will, freedom is not the bare ability to pick between options but the capacity to pursue what is good, so that a choice against the good is a failure of freedom rather than an exercise of it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This makes free will an evaluative rather than a metaphysical notion, which is why the ancient treatments of the problem look so unlike the modern ones.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Plato holds the view in this form: to be free is to be governed by the rational part of the soul, and the person ruled by appetite is enslaved even when unobstructed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a><ul><li>The psychology that this conception of freedom presupposes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Some evidence that suggests that it is more and more difficult for children to be able to rise and earn more than their parents has seen a dramatic decline in the US over the last half-century.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This supports the idea that income inequality is going to worsen.</p><h4>Evidence</h4><ul><li>For children born in 1940, 90% earned more than their parents, but for those born in the 1980s, that number plummets to just 50%.</li><li>Most of this decline was from the industrial Midwest.</li><li>It was primarily caused by the unequal distribution on the GDP growth, not the fact that there was not GDP growth.</li></ul><h5>Intergenerational Mobility</h5><ul><li>Economists have found a “Great Gatsby curve” whereby countries such as the US or UK, where there is relatively higher income inequality, have lower intergenerational mobility compared to Denmark or Norway, where there is much higher income mobility.</li><li>There have theorized to be several causes for this:<ul><li>Richer families are able to have greater human capital investment in their children, ensuring that their children have more educational resources and thus more human capital.</li><li>Societies with a higher “college premium” tend to have lower mobility.</li><li>Rich people have more connections and can engage in nepotism.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>Defending the One Percent</em><ul><li>Contrasts with the above note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>While this article does not attack the idea of meritocracy, it provide evidence that meritocracy is failing.</li></ul></li></ul>","Innovation in fields such as green energy and pharmaceuticals are slowing down":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The rate of pharmaceutical innovation and green energy innovation has slowed, producing what is known as “Eroom’s Law” (reverse of Moore’s Law).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potentially dangerous trend, because it means that productivity growth in the pharmaceutical sector is slowing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are four causes for specifically pharmacuticals:</p><ol><li>Increasing competition from generic brand drugs.<ol><li>It is difficult for newly created drugs to compete effectively against generic brand drugs that often achieve the same purpose, but are priced far lower.</li></ol></li><li>Increasingly stricter regulations.<ol><li>Over time, the regulation surrounding the development of new drugs accumulate and the barrier becomes more difficult to overcome, since there are now higher standards.</li></ol></li><li>An increasing amount of money is being spent.<ol><li>In a stochastic process, more money does not mean greater efficiency.</li></ol></li><li>A bias towards “reductionism.”<ol><li>A trend towards looking at things through molecular perspective, rather than a more macro perspective. But this doesn’t really translate to the creation of new drugs.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Ultimately, it comes down to the fact that there are increasing restrictions on the ability of firms to innovate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is also a reason for the problem described in this note?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation\">Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation</a><ul><li>Is this the case here?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas are getting harder to find\">Ideas are getting harder to find</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This note provides an example in support of this point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>For instance, regulation my slow down innovation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>If there is a lack of innovation then, it would also mean more entrenched advantages for existing players in the fields described in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A polypeptide chain’s N-terminus is created first, so new amino acids are attached via peptide bonds on the C-terminus.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that might appear on an AP Bio test. This is also analogous to how New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"New nucleotides are added to the 3&#x27; end of a DNA or RNA\">New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA</a></li></ul>","One must discard knowledge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That in order to truly become enlightened, one must discard knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core procedures in Taoism for one to achieve enlightenment. Same thing in Zen Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Taoism</h5><p>The ordinary use of knowledge is to make distinctions. However, in order to truly become a part of the cosmos, the individual must abandon concepts of differentiation and embrace the undifferentiated aesthetic continuum. A further distinction must be made between having <em>no-knowledge</em> and <em>having-no</em> knowledge. The latter is a sort of innocence that is innate to children. The former, however, is the goal of the sage, who must discard the knowledge that they have accumulated to return to a sort of enlightened innocence.</p><h5>Zen</h5><p>When one comes to be able to perceive the Tao is when one has achieved enlightenment. This occurs when the individual loses their knowledge of the distinction between the knower and the known. Therefore, one while one is not without knowledge, the only type of knowledge one then possess is non-knowledge; it is a state in which all distinctions are gone.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao\">By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao</a><ul><li>In order for this to occur, one must first abandon knowledge.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a><ul><li>It is when we lose all our knowledge that Sudden Enlightenment becomes possible.</li></ul></li></ul>","People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Training in the arts should be something that is required of all youth, since without such training, they are aesthetically inexperienced, and thus are both unable to attain full virtue and also fully enjoy the pleasures which result from the arts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle makes this point in the Politics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>According to Aristotle, the citizenry should be trained to play an instrument that is not to difficult (those instruments are reserved for professionals, who are slaves) so that they are able to cultivate their own enjoyment of art. Aristotle specifically argues that people should be educated in music, since music is most able to improve the soul (it arouses strong emotions in the viewer, and is most capable of changing one’s dispositions and thoughts).</p><ul><li>Music also has therapeutic and pleasure-inducing purposes.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul\">Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul</a><ul><li>This is the reason Aristotle choses music as the thing to educate the youth with.</li></ul></li></ul>","Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the most important things for an individual to understand is the relative scarcities of the goods that he is seeking. Knowing this, he is able to choose the optimal bundle of goods which maximize its utility or minimizes his average total cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental purpose of the price system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This feature of the price system is what makes it valuable. No other system is able to propagate information about the availability of various goods or capital as quickly and efficiently as the price system.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>The Use of Knowledge in Society</em>, Hayek</p><p>The whole acts as one market, not because any of its members survey the whole field, but because <strong>their limited individual fields of vision sufficiently overlap so that through many intermediaries the relevant information is communicated to all</strong>. The mere fact that there is one price for any commodity—or rather that local prices are connected in a manner determined by the cost of transport, etc.—brings about the solution which (it is just conceptually possible) might have been arrived at by one single mind possessing all the information which is in fact dispersed among all the people involved in the process.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking\">Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking</a><ul><li>This is the reason why when the price signal is distorted, that misallocation of resources occur.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Socialism seeks to centralize the power of the market in a central planner, but they are unable to aggregate all the information in a market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>Mises states that it is not even that a socialist state can’t aggregate the information, but that the information is produced by the market. Without the market, there is no information.</li></ul></li></ul>","Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In many different religions/belief systems, the idea of reincarnation is an important facet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a connection between many different belief system in the world, helping us find similarities between otherwise disparate ideas.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In Hinduism, As the human soul migrated from body to body across lifetimes, <em>karma</em>, depending on the \"pureness\" of one's actions, may allow oneself to be reborn to a higher caste, eventually, to achieve Brahman (see The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman\">The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman</a></li></ul>","Social contract theories place heavy epistemic demands on the parties to the contract":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Both Hobbes’ and Locke’s theories of government are incredibly demanding with regards to their necessary epistemic assumptions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves to weaken both theories of the origins/functions of government.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One of the core assumptions of Hobbes’ and Locke’s theories is their assumption of a “state of nature,” whereby humans are wholly unorganized and in constant conflict (Hobbes) or in harmony (Locke). This is already a heavy epistemic assumption. There is no empirical evidence to support even the existence of a “state of nature,” as humans have always been organized in structures beyond the individual. In addition to this, there are further assumptions such as that the establishment of a government would actually solve the problems that the two thinkers identify in terms of preventing conflicting or ensuring certain rights.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a><ul><li>A similar thing is probably true of Confucianist theories of state too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a><ul><li>This is essentially Hobbes’ view.</li></ul></li></ul>","Status functions are created by declaration speech acts, which in turn, creates institutional facts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Declaration speech acts are statements that announce the creation of institutional fact, a linguistic representation of status function (a piece of paper can be representative of value). This status function then creates institutional facts (a piece of green paper with $20 written on it is representative of $20 of value).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the existence of institutional facts in the world, and connects to the underlying, “empirical” reality.</p><h4>Institutional Reality Constructed</h4><p>Status functions are first constructed through the formula “$X$ counts as $Y$ in context $C$ .” This process is facilitated with a declarative speech act, which is the linguistic act that serves as the superstructure of the status function. $Y$ then becomes an institutional fact (once socially accepted), a state of affairs that is socially accepted as being objectively true. Declarative speech acts do not describe reality, but rather create reality within a shared context.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts\">Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts</a></li></ul>","The Ayodhya Dispute between Hindus and Muslims mirror’s India struggle with identity, secularism, and historical memory":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Ayodhya dispute centers on a plot of land in Ayodhya where both Hindus and Muslims claims is a religious site. Although the issue has largely been settled by 2020, it serves as an indicator of religious tensions in India before that.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The event highlights conflicts over religion in India, and disputes the claim that due to Hinduism’s tolerance for other belief systems, that there would not be any religious conflict.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li></ul>","The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Japanese model of railroads have been particularly successful not because of culture specifically, but because of the unique business model of the Japanese rail companies, and the restraint of the government when it comes to regulation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can serve as an example other nations would follow when it comes to creating a well-ordered rail system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The success of rail in Japan doesn't have to do with culture.</li><li>On the national scale, there are six large private rail networks as a part of the Japanese Rail (JR) system.</li><li>They all have a unique business model where they don't just operate trains. They also have a bunch of side businesses that generate a lot of revenue. In fact, the railroad business (although it is almost always profitable) usually generates only a plurality or small majority of the revenue.<ul><li>They essentially serve as city builders, where they develop rural land with train lines and houses and stuff (that they construct), and thus (1) make the land valuable, (2) provide a guaranteed consumer base for their train, and (3) provide from real estate and other businesses that they then run.</li><li>To make this work they also have smart land use where a lot of the land was reserved for parks and forests and stuff, to increase living quality.</li></ul></li><li>Railroads generally work the best in areas of high urban density, since they have high throughput.</li><li>Overall, Japanese cities are not actually as dense as their European counterparts or cities in Korea.<ul><li>What makes them unique is their hyperdense centers.</li></ul></li><li>What allowed for the development of this is their liberal development control systems compared to the West.<ul><li>It is really easy to do land adjustment in Japan, since all it takes is that 2/3 of residents and landowners agree, and they can replan the land.</li></ul></li><li>There is not an implicit subsidy for car ownership in Japan like in the US.<ul><li>Japan has private parking (people can't park on public land), and someone who buys a car must first prove that they have private land where they can park their car.</li></ul></li><li>Vertical integration of the Japanese train companies ensures that companies are run efficiently.</li><li>The generous price cap on train fares ensure that companies actually make a profit, and thus actually have an incentive to maximize ridership.</li><li>They can also make their own decisions about timetables, service patterns, and day-to-day operations.<ul><li>In other countries, this is inefficiently done by government.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>This is perhaps what is happening in Japan.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Third Front allowed for the Reform and Opening Up to occur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Third Front is a period of massive infrastructure development which spread human and physical capital around the nation, ultimately making the Reform and Opening Up possible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The Reform and Opening Up, an important moment in Chinese history, was only possible due to the preexisting infrastructure that is constructed during this period of time.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>The reason that China was able to succeed in this project is due to their strong centralized state.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Tragedy of Great Power Politics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Argues that because the international system has no central authority and states can never trust each other's intentions, great powers are driven by fear to maximize their power and seek hegemony as the only sure path to survival. The result is tragic and unavoidable: even states that want only security are forced to compete aggressively, making lasting peace impossible. He calls this theory \"offensive realism,\" tests it against two centuries of great-power history, and concludes that China cannot rise peacefully—destining it and the U.S. to become rivals.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main branches of realism that I think carries with it less epistemically demanding assumptions about the nature of humanity and also provides a relatively accurate overall picture of competition among states.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>The Fundamentals</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The international system forces states to be aggressive with each other\">The international system forces states to be aggressive with each other</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great powers incessantly strive to become the hegemon\">Great powers incessantly strive to become the hegemon</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The internal characteristics of a state does not matter for international relations\">The internal characteristics of a state does not matter for international relations</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade brings people into contact, not agreement\">Trade brings people into contact, not agreement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American moralism sets it most in line with a liberal theory of international relations\">American moralism sets it most in line with a liberal theory of international relations</a><ul><li>What are some theories that explain the origins of American exceptionalism/the ideosyncratic moralism of the American people?</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Reading Plan</h4><p><strong>Must read (the core argument)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ch. 1, Introduction.</strong> The thesis and how offensive realism differs from Waltz’s defensive realism. Table 1.1 is the map of the whole field.</li><li><strong>Ch. 2, Anarchy and the Struggle for Power.</strong> The five assumptions and how they lead to “states maximize relative power.” This is the book. Read it slowly and attack each step.</li><li><strong>Ch. 3, Wealth and Power.</strong> This is the economics-to-security link, which matters most for your interest in international economic policy. Latent power (wealth, population) is separate from military power. This is why a rich China worries him.</li><li><strong>Ch. 10, Great Power Politics in the Twenty-First Century.</strong> His predictions, including Table 10.3 on China’s economy. This is where you test the theory against 25 years of evidence.</li></ul><p><strong>Worth reading</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ch. 6, Great Powers in Action.</strong> The Japan case (1868–1945, Map 6.1) is the most relevant to East Asia. Germany and the USSR are good too.</li><li><strong>Ch. 4, The Primacy of Land Power.</strong> “The stopping power of water” explains why he thinks the US cannot be a global hegemon and acts as an offshore balancer. You need it to follow Ch. 7 and Ch. 10.</li></ul><p><strong>Skim or skip</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ch. 5, Strategies for Survival.</strong> Mostly taxonomy (balancing, buck-passing, bait-and-bleed). Read the definitions and move on.</li><li><strong>Ch. 7, The Offshore Balancers.</strong> The US and UK cases. Useful, but the argument is predictable once you have Ch. 4.</li><li><strong>Ch. 8, Balancing versus Buck-Passing,</strong> and <strong>Ch. 9, The Causes of Great Power War.</strong> Long empirical testing. Read Ch. 9’s conclusion (Table 9.3) for the claim that unbalanced multipolarity is the most war-prone system, and skip the rest.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The dominance of the world in the past by the West was the result of superior technology, and not the result of superior values or technology, as some have claimed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the idea that the West's culture is superior compared to the culture of other civilizations.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>But there is no evidence that the reason that the West had superior technology is not because their value/religion promoted the development of such things?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies\">Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies</a><ul><li>If this note is the case, it could mean that China (due to their more positive view of new technology) could come to dominate the world in the future.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>If it was actually superior values, it seems that people would not then reject these values.</li></ul></li></ul>","The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that moral virtue is not about having the right end goal, it is about how one chooses to attain that goal, the actions that they undertake.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This ties into Aristotle’s larger philosophy regarding what is considered virtuous and how we attain virtue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In this way, deliberation and choice are required in order for someone to be virtuous, since it is the job of deliberation to decide on the means, and it is choice which allows us to enact the means that we decide upon.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>When we exercise a moral virtue, it ought to be me in the mean between two excesses.</li></ul></li></ul>","The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proponents of Georgism argue that thee land value tax is the best form of tax since it only taxes what is not the result of human investment and labor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core component of schools of thought like Georgism or Geolibertarianism, who argue that all other forms of tax are misdirected and unequal.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Proponents argue that taxing land is effective since it is not the product of human labor, and merely a part of nature, which they consider to be owned by no one/all of us. Additionally, since a tax would ensure that land does not go to waste, as people simply sit on their land doing nothing as the value of land slowly increases. It incentivizes people to make the best use of their land and make sure that it is productive enough to offset the taxes on the land itself. Otherwise, it will be sold to someone who can better make use of the land. However, this is premised on the idea that land is somehow communally owned, or that it cannot be the result of private property. Such a view emphasizes the fact that people should be not subject to any taxation, including land, since it violates the property rights of the landowner (this is mostly an Austrian view). </p><ul><li>The LVT is a tax that does not have any dead weight loss, since supply is perfectly inelastic.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>With a LVT, this type of behavior would be avoided, since it be very difficult to avoid a tax on land by engaging in rent-seeking behavior.</li></ul></li></ul>","The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The polarization that is caused by electrostatic induction is very much similar to a polar molecule described in chemistry.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Since both objects polarized by electrostatic induction and polar objects in chemistry are <em>polar,</em> they share similar traits. For example, they are both overall neutral, but the polarized regions may experience a electromagnetic force pulling it towards the opposite charge.</p>","The state should do as little as possible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that the state, for whatever reason, should do nothing at all, or act very minimally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is present throughout many different strands of thought, including Taoism and Libertarianism.</p><h4>Laozi</h4><p>Since Laozi believes that knowledge is bad and people should live according to the Tao, and the state represents a means by which the natural course of things is disrupted, that the state should do as little as possible. When the Taoist sage comes to power, he should first dismantle the preexisting rules and regulations, and then govern with “non-action.”</p><ul><li>The ruler should do nothing and let the people do what they can themselves.</li></ul><p>Zhuangzi makes a similar argument, though for him, he highlights that the very artificial nature of the state is something which goes against the Tao, and thus should be reduced/destroyed.</p><ul><li>He also talk about how the governance of the state is not able to take into account the diverse interests of the people, and thus, any attempt at governing merely increases human misery.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is similar to what Zhuangzi emphasizes.</li></ul></li></ul>","The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Typically, the things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do, since they are usually more pleasurable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle makes an important point that the things that we most want to do are often things that bring us farther away from the mean.</p><h4>Aristotle’s Guide</h4><p>Aristotle says that to find the mean:</p><ol><li>Keep away from the side (in the spectrum between the two extremes of some action) that is more contrary to the mean.</li><li>Notice our own errors and do the opposite.</li><li>Guard against pleasure.</li></ol><p>But he says that getting to the mean is ultimately up to the individual, and that there is no general philosophical principle that can guide someone to the mean in their life. It just our own intuition that has to do the work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>We must constantly and often do things that are against what we might otherwise want to do.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>To act in a virtuous state means, in practice, to act in the manner described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>To achieve happiness, then, means to act in a way that gets us as far away from the side that is more opposed to the mean as possible.</li></ul></li></ul>","The universality of history is a product of Christian history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The concept of universality in history is something that originates in Christianity, can thus cannot be applied in a context that is outside of Christianity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The idea of universality is something that is unique to Christianity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Universal salvation and other such concepts which present Christianity as something that is universal impacts all of humankind is something that is unique to Christianity and something that does not exist naturally in the world or in many other religions. Therefore, attempts to create “universal” theories of history outside of the Christian framework is impossible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>Related to this, especially is a post-Christian world continues to maintain many of the aspects of universalism introduced first by Christianity.</li></ul></li></ul>","Trade brings people into contact, not agreement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Trade brings people into contract, but it does not necessarily mean that people will be brought into agreement.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the narrative presented by the Doux Commerce thesis.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Believers of this theory are largely also believers of the liberal theory of International relations, who thinks that the key to stability is having high levels of economic interdependence between states. For instance, there is a persistent cultural divide between Australia and the rest of Asian (which is its biggest trading partner). Although they have close trading ties, their diplomatic relations remain tense, and there is no acceptance of Australia as an \"Asian country\" among the actual \"Asian\" countries.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a><ul><li>This holds true given the contents of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>These notes are contradictory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is a counterexample to the ideas presented in this note?</li></ul></li></ul>","Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A wealth tax may create an incentive to shelter wealth in the firm, leading to greater intangible asset and human capital investments, though this does not apply to physical capital investments.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potential argument in favor of establishing a wealth tax, given the right kind of barriers and regulations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Obviously, this tax would only be effective if place upon business owners that have the ability to readily investment their capital. This effect is only present when the tax represents less than 5% of liquid assets of the taxpayer. If the tax is increased to more than 20%, then the effect becomes negative as owners begin to extract capital from their business in order to pay the tax. For the average firm owner in Norway, the portfolio reallocation effect (i.e., investing more into the firm) is stronger than the liquidity constraint (i.e., taking capital out of the business). The wealth tax also means that owners will work more, since they fell like they are relatively poorer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight\">Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight</a><ul><li>This is a related idea of taxing the wealthy/capital owners, and both this and that note identify potentially overall beneficial ways of conducting such a tax.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>A related idea, which seems to only be true when the tax is greater than 20% of the individual liquid assets, at least in the case of business owners and through the results of this study.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
